WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.270 --> 00:00:05.110
I'm Jenna Fisher.

2
00:00:05.270 --> 00:00:06.410
And I'm Angela Kinsey.

3
00:00:06.550 --> 00:00:07.690
We were on The Office together.

4
00:00:07.930 --> 00:00:08.950
And we're best friends.

5
00:00:09.050 --> 00:00:12.770
And now we're doing the Ultimate Office rewatch podcast just for you.

6
00:00:12.930 --> 00:00:19.750
Each week, we will break down an episode of The Office and give exclusive behind-the-scenes stories that only two people who were there can tell you.

7
00:00:19.910 --> 00:00:20.910
We're The Office ladies.

8
00:00:25.760 --> 00:00:26.680
Hey there.

9
00:00:27.460 --> 00:00:28.300
How's it going?

10
00:00:29.020 --> 00:00:30.840
You know, it's going fantastic.

11
00:00:31.380 --> 00:00:31.740
Uh-huh.

12
00:00:31.940 --> 00:00:33.560
I'm ready for some trivia.

13
00:00:33.840 --> 00:00:36.400
I hope so, because that's what we're talking about today.

14
00:00:36.400 --> 00:00:40.940
Season 8, Episode 11, written by Steve Healy and directed by B.J.

15
00:00:41.040 --> 00:00:41.420
Novak.

16
00:00:41.720 --> 00:00:42.600
How about a summary?

17
00:00:42.920 --> 00:00:44.120
You know I love it.

18
00:00:44.940 --> 00:00:51.320
Andy recruits the gang to compete in a trivia night in hopes that their winnings will close the gap on their quarterly sales goal.

19
00:00:51.760 --> 00:00:56.120
Meanwhile, Dwight flies to Florida in pursuit of a management position.

20
00:00:56.420 --> 00:00:56.740
Uh-huh.

21
00:00:57.440 --> 00:00:58.680
Very bold move.

22
00:00:59.060 --> 00:01:02.620
I mean, Robert did invite him, but I think Robert invites people.

23
00:01:02.800 --> 00:01:03.920
I mean, much like Joe Bennett.

24
00:01:04.140 --> 00:01:04.400
Uh-huh.

25
00:01:04.400 --> 00:01:07.900
Maybe sometimes invite you, but they don't really mean the invite.

26
00:01:08.360 --> 00:01:10.000
Yeah, it's like a soft invite.

27
00:01:10.240 --> 00:01:10.700
I think it's a—

28
00:01:10.700 --> 00:01:11.680
What do you call it?

29
00:01:11.800 --> 00:01:13.600
I don't even think it's a soft invite.

30
00:01:13.800 --> 00:01:14.740
It's a—

31
00:01:14.740 --> 00:01:16.820
I think it's a non-invite invite.

32
00:01:17.240 --> 00:01:18.620
A hypothetical invite.

33
00:01:18.740 --> 00:01:19.120
Yes.

34
00:01:19.340 --> 00:01:19.580
Okay.

35
00:01:20.080 --> 00:01:20.380
Yeah.

36
00:01:20.380 --> 00:01:22.220
Dwight doesn't live in hypotheticals.

37
00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:23.000
No.

38
00:01:23.260 --> 00:01:23.620
No.

39
00:01:23.620 --> 00:01:32.700
Well, fast fact number one, or perhaps I should call it trivia number one, because this will be a little trivia for all of you office lovers.

40
00:01:32.920 --> 00:01:35.520
This episode was originally called Clicks.

41
00:01:35.780 --> 00:01:36.420
Oh, yeah.

42
00:01:36.880 --> 00:01:37.060
Yeah.

43
00:01:37.340 --> 00:01:38.040
Called Clicks.

44
00:01:38.160 --> 00:01:38.440
Yeah.

45
00:01:38.920 --> 00:01:41.500
Well, I still have my copy of Clicks.

46
00:01:41.520 --> 00:01:42.140
Look at you.

47
00:01:42.140 --> 00:01:43.260
I re-read it.

48
00:01:43.580 --> 00:01:43.630
Look at you.

49
00:01:43.780 --> 00:01:44.480
That's right.

50
00:01:44.660 --> 00:01:45.800
Talk about digital clutter.

51
00:01:46.040 --> 00:01:48.360
Who's showing up today with the digital clutter?

52
00:01:48.560 --> 00:01:49.120
You are.

53
00:01:49.580 --> 00:01:50.800
Well, it's very different.

54
00:01:51.200 --> 00:01:55.300
First of all, the storyline with Dwight going to interview in Florida, it's all in there.

55
00:01:55.460 --> 00:01:56.640
That was the same.

56
00:01:56.900 --> 00:01:57.100
Okay.

57
00:01:57.100 --> 00:01:59.840
But back at the office, here's what happens.

58
00:02:00.420 --> 00:02:05.900
Kelly finds out that Kathy and Aaron hung out over the weekend, and she is not having it.

59
00:02:06.100 --> 00:02:07.380
Oh, she was left out.

60
00:02:08.680 --> 00:02:14.060
She goes to Andy, and she says, there are Clicks in this office, and you need to do something about it.

61
00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:19.060
We can't have people hanging out outside of work, gossiping about work.

62
00:02:19.140 --> 00:02:20.800
Although Kelly's never cared before.

63
00:02:21.020 --> 00:02:21.240
No.

64
00:02:21.500 --> 00:02:21.700
No.

65
00:02:22.200 --> 00:02:24.680
So Andy calls a group meeting to talk about it.

66
00:02:24.680 --> 00:02:29.740
And, you know, he says, listen, it's not against the rules to hang out with people outside of work,

67
00:02:29.740 --> 00:02:34.680
but you can only do it in a way that doesn't exclude people, and you can't gossip about work.

68
00:02:35.300 --> 00:02:36.640
And then he gives an example.

69
00:02:37.060 --> 00:02:40.820
He says, for example, this Saturday, I went to Jim and Pam's house,

70
00:02:41.300 --> 00:02:45.140
but it wasn't a click because there was no gossiping, and then I left.

71
00:02:45.340 --> 00:02:47.180
And Aaron is like, oh, my God.

72
00:02:47.640 --> 00:02:49.840
I went to Jim and Pam's house on Saturday.

73
00:02:49.840 --> 00:02:51.000
I must have just missed you.

74
00:02:51.140 --> 00:02:52.180
I was there for dinner.

75
00:02:52.640 --> 00:02:53.560
But guess what?

76
00:02:53.820 --> 00:02:54.020
What?

77
00:02:54.020 --> 00:02:55.900
But Daryl was also there for dinner.

78
00:02:56.100 --> 00:02:58.520
And Kathy was there for dinner.

79
00:02:59.320 --> 00:03:00.740
At Jim and Pam's house?

80
00:03:00.820 --> 00:03:01.200
Yes.

81
00:03:01.360 --> 00:03:03.860
They invited everybody over to meet the baby.

82
00:03:04.060 --> 00:03:07.400
And they explained, listen, we weren't trying to exclude people,

83
00:03:07.500 --> 00:03:11.500
but these are the people who expressed interest in meeting our baby, so we had them over.

84
00:03:11.600 --> 00:03:14.820
If you had expressed interest in meeting the baby, you could have come over, too.

85
00:03:15.360 --> 00:03:19.100
Suddenly, everybody starts confessing who they hang out with outside of work.

86
00:03:19.100 --> 00:03:25.440
And this is when it is revealed that Oscar, Ryan, Jim, and Toby are on a trivia team together.

87
00:03:26.360 --> 00:03:29.940
This is where trivia starts to make its way in to clicks.

88
00:03:30.720 --> 00:03:35.980
And he decides that the best way to break down office clicks is for them to all form a trivia team together.

89
00:03:35.980 --> 00:03:40.380
And they're going to play as the Dunder Mifflin, Scranton trivia team.

90
00:03:40.960 --> 00:03:42.080
So they do.

91
00:03:42.080 --> 00:03:46.080
So there was a trivia element to clicks, but it was only at the very end.

92
00:03:46.500 --> 00:03:53.200
I got in touch with writer Steve Healy, and he said that after we did the table read for clicks, they got a network note.

93
00:03:53.440 --> 00:03:57.060
That network said, we don't understand this idea of clicks.

94
00:03:57.080 --> 00:03:59.260
We don't understand what they would mean to the office.

95
00:03:59.400 --> 00:04:00.640
It's not clear to us.

96
00:04:00.640 --> 00:04:01.740
We don't get it.

97
00:04:01.820 --> 00:04:03.180
We don't get it, basically.

98
00:04:03.720 --> 00:04:05.280
They were like, we don't get it.

99
00:04:06.440 --> 00:04:13.560
So Steve Healy and Paul Lieberstein, they kind of agreed, and they did this massive reimagining of this A storyline.

100
00:04:13.840 --> 00:04:16.740
And they decided, let's just lean into the trivia night part.

101
00:04:16.900 --> 00:04:18.320
Like, that part was so fun.

102
00:04:18.380 --> 00:04:19.440
The network liked that part.

103
00:04:19.540 --> 00:04:20.580
They were like, we get that.

104
00:04:20.660 --> 00:04:21.560
We get trivia night.

105
00:04:21.560 --> 00:04:21.760
Yeah.

106
00:04:21.920 --> 00:04:24.800
And that is how it became the focus of the episode.

107
00:04:24.940 --> 00:04:25.340
Oh.

108
00:04:25.760 --> 00:04:27.440
But they only had a week to do that.

109
00:04:27.540 --> 00:04:28.480
Yeah, it's a big rewrite.

110
00:04:28.480 --> 00:04:33.420
We did the table read, and one week later, we started shooting, and we get this new script called Trivia.

111
00:04:33.600 --> 00:04:34.100
We're like, what?

112
00:04:34.340 --> 00:04:34.580
What?

113
00:04:35.060 --> 00:04:35.660
There you go.

114
00:04:35.900 --> 00:04:36.660
I like that.

115
00:04:37.220 --> 00:04:37.600
All right.

116
00:04:37.600 --> 00:04:38.780
Good fast fact.

117
00:04:38.940 --> 00:04:39.480
Thank you.

118
00:04:39.700 --> 00:04:40.720
Our trivia fact.

119
00:04:41.220 --> 00:04:42.660
Trivia fact number two.

120
00:04:42.860 --> 00:04:43.100
Okay.

121
00:04:43.560 --> 00:04:54.040
Steve Healy, our writer, told me that he was very excited to lean into trivia because he used to play a lot of bar trivia when he lived in New York City back in 2003.

122
00:04:54.480 --> 00:05:01.340
He said it was just sweeping the East Village and the area around NYU where he lived, and he said he would go with his roommate,

123
00:05:01.520 --> 00:05:06.200
and they took it super seriously because the prize was a $25 bar tab.

124
00:05:06.440 --> 00:05:06.760
Hey.

125
00:05:07.060 --> 00:05:07.420
Yeah.

126
00:05:07.700 --> 00:05:07.820
Yeah.

127
00:05:07.820 --> 00:05:08.980
He was like, we needed that.

128
00:05:09.160 --> 00:05:09.360
Yeah.

129
00:05:09.700 --> 00:05:12.800
He said they also had a couple of ringers that they would bring along.

130
00:05:13.320 --> 00:05:17.780
They had this friend who was a super smart attorney who would come out once a month for the big game.

131
00:05:17.880 --> 00:05:18.600
How fun.

132
00:05:18.600 --> 00:05:20.740
I want to be the ringer at Trivia Night.

133
00:05:20.740 --> 00:05:23.240
I mean, it will never happen, but that's so cool.

134
00:05:23.440 --> 00:05:27.520
He said this was fun because he got to write what he knew.

135
00:05:27.840 --> 00:05:28.160
Oh.

136
00:05:28.600 --> 00:05:32.740
And I always love when the scripts come from someone's personal experience.

137
00:05:33.400 --> 00:05:34.040
All right.

138
00:05:34.180 --> 00:05:41.640
Well, speaking of trivia nights, fast fact number three, Angela, you said you did a little digging around about office trivia nights.

139
00:05:41.760 --> 00:05:42.020
Yeah.

140
00:05:42.260 --> 00:05:43.560
Which are very popular.

141
00:05:43.780 --> 00:05:45.020
They're super popular.

142
00:05:45.020 --> 00:05:52.640
You can basically look up your city and type in the office trivia night near me, and things are going to come popping up.

143
00:05:52.880 --> 00:05:53.000
Yeah.

144
00:05:53.240 --> 00:05:53.680
Trust me.

145
00:05:53.980 --> 00:06:03.100
I did one for my area, and Jenna, coming up not too far from us is a painting with a twist trivia night.

146
00:06:03.320 --> 00:06:04.220
Wait, wait, wait, wait, wait, wait, wait.

147
00:06:04.440 --> 00:06:07.560
Am I going to paint a picture while doing office trivia?

148
00:06:07.800 --> 00:06:08.120
Uh-huh.

149
00:06:08.120 --> 00:06:10.020
It is really popular.

150
00:06:10.340 --> 00:06:14.380
It's called Paper Company Trivia, and on their website it says,

151
00:06:14.580 --> 00:06:21.780
bring your office coworkers and your world's best boss mug and test your office knowledge at this paper company-themed trivia night.

152
00:06:22.060 --> 00:06:24.780
Everyone paints the Dunder Mifflin office building.

153
00:06:26.220 --> 00:06:27.340
I want us to go.

154
00:06:27.500 --> 00:06:28.080
I know.

155
00:06:28.320 --> 00:06:30.180
We have to show up at this thing one night.

156
00:06:30.180 --> 00:06:30.600
I'm telling you.

157
00:06:31.380 --> 00:06:33.260
The sad part is I think we'll lose.

158
00:06:33.760 --> 00:06:37.000
Even though we've been doing that, we're doing good since we've done this rewatch.

159
00:06:37.000 --> 00:06:37.220
We're much better.

160
00:06:37.500 --> 00:06:39.600
Remember our trivia with Billie Eilish?

161
00:06:39.720 --> 00:06:40.780
I was a stinker.

162
00:06:41.000 --> 00:06:42.560
You did not do well that day.

163
00:06:42.620 --> 00:06:43.180
You did horribly.

164
00:06:43.280 --> 00:06:45.680
I did horrible when we did it on the Today Show.

165
00:06:45.840 --> 00:06:46.380
I froze.

166
00:06:46.700 --> 00:06:47.160
I know.

167
00:06:47.300 --> 00:06:48.120
This is the problem.

168
00:06:48.920 --> 00:06:53.540
We could freeze, but we would also be painting and having a glass of wine, I think.

169
00:06:53.740 --> 00:06:55.500
How many people do you need for a team?

170
00:06:55.700 --> 00:06:56.260
I don't know.

171
00:06:56.260 --> 00:06:56.820
Does it say?

172
00:06:56.820 --> 00:06:57.220
All right.

173
00:06:57.300 --> 00:06:58.160
I will dig deeper.

174
00:06:58.160 --> 00:07:02.060
I just was very excited that we got to paint and do office trivia.

175
00:07:02.320 --> 00:07:06.580
We'll find out because I think we could maybe recruit a couple of other office mates.

176
00:07:06.900 --> 00:07:08.380
Oh, I bet Kate would go with us.

177
00:07:08.840 --> 00:07:10.220
And Oscar would be good.

178
00:07:10.360 --> 00:07:11.000
Probably Creed.

179
00:07:11.240 --> 00:07:15.120
Creed, you know, came to my 50th birthday painting party.

180
00:07:15.300 --> 00:07:15.460
Yeah.

181
00:07:15.520 --> 00:07:16.780
Where we painted hummingbirds.

182
00:07:17.040 --> 00:07:21.060
And he painted a little alien ship with his hummingbirds.

183
00:07:21.260 --> 00:07:21.640
Yeah, I remember.

184
00:07:21.980 --> 00:07:23.440
We were like, wait, where are the aliens?

185
00:07:23.680 --> 00:07:26.000
Well, you know who'd be horrible at a trivia night is Rainn Wilson.

186
00:07:26.000 --> 00:07:28.760
And Rainn, I'm sorry to say this, but you never remember anything.

187
00:07:29.080 --> 00:07:29.980
You're not wrong.

188
00:07:30.180 --> 00:07:34.420
I'm like, oh my gosh, Rainn, remember that episode where you get hit by the car and you

189
00:07:34.420 --> 00:07:35.340
throw up on the hood?

190
00:07:35.460 --> 00:07:36.200
He's like, what?

191
00:07:36.880 --> 00:07:38.320
I'll have to rewatch it.

192
00:07:38.680 --> 00:07:39.000
Yeah.

193
00:07:39.620 --> 00:07:42.420
Once he rewatches it, he gets all these ideas.

194
00:07:42.420 --> 00:07:45.100
But like, he has no trivia knowledge of The Office.

195
00:07:45.380 --> 00:07:45.680
None.

196
00:07:45.960 --> 00:07:47.240
Brian Baumgartner might be good.

197
00:07:47.340 --> 00:07:48.580
Brian would kick some butt.

198
00:07:48.580 --> 00:07:56.140
And then also, you guys, if you love Office trivia, Dave Koechner is hosting Trivia Nights.

199
00:07:56.320 --> 00:08:02.240
He travels around to different bars and we'll put the swipe up in our stories, but it says,

200
00:08:02.440 --> 00:08:06.840
test your knowledge of The Office with the Pac-Man himself, David Koechner.

201
00:08:07.280 --> 00:08:12.540
Teams compete for prizes while also hearing behind the scenes stories from David about playing

202
00:08:12.540 --> 00:08:15.560
the hit show's favorite obnoxious paper salesman.

203
00:08:16.240 --> 00:08:17.500
That's pretty cool.

204
00:08:17.500 --> 00:08:18.080
I know.

205
00:08:18.600 --> 00:08:20.560
Well, listen, why don't we take a break?

206
00:08:20.560 --> 00:08:23.960
And when we get back, we're going to have total silence.

207
00:08:24.540 --> 00:08:27.420
We won't speak for 14 minutes.

208
00:08:28.140 --> 00:08:29.700
Enjoy that in your commute.

209
00:08:30.680 --> 00:08:31.900
We'll be right back.

210
00:08:41.980 --> 00:08:43.600
We are back from break.

211
00:08:43.840 --> 00:08:49.020
While we were on break, we decided our trivia team is going to be our Office Ladies team.

212
00:08:49.320 --> 00:08:50.900
Sam, Cassie, get ready.

213
00:08:51.240 --> 00:08:55.880
Polish up your The Office trivia and get your paintbrushes because I think we've all got to

214
00:08:55.880 --> 00:08:56.120
go.

215
00:08:56.360 --> 00:08:56.480
We're ready.

216
00:08:56.480 --> 00:08:58.960
Listen, they watch it every week just like we do.

217
00:08:59.100 --> 00:08:59.360
I know.

218
00:08:59.480 --> 00:09:00.060
This is the team.

219
00:09:00.200 --> 00:09:01.660
We might actually win now.

220
00:09:01.860 --> 00:09:02.680
Team Office Ladies.

221
00:09:02.840 --> 00:09:03.020
Yeah.

222
00:09:03.360 --> 00:09:04.000
I'm excited.

223
00:09:04.180 --> 00:09:04.800
Oh, my gosh.

224
00:09:04.840 --> 00:09:06.820
This is so crazy if we really did that.

225
00:09:07.100 --> 00:09:08.040
That's so crazy.

226
00:09:08.200 --> 00:09:08.700
We're going to do it.

227
00:09:08.700 --> 00:09:09.480
What if we won?

228
00:09:10.280 --> 00:09:11.160
That would be...

229
00:09:11.160 --> 00:09:13.220
I feel like people would be angry.

230
00:09:13.460 --> 00:09:14.840
I feel like that's not fair.

231
00:09:15.060 --> 00:09:15.860
It's rigged.

232
00:09:16.820 --> 00:09:17.520
All right.

233
00:09:17.700 --> 00:09:21.420
Listen, this cold open is very, very, very, very quiet.

234
00:09:21.620 --> 00:09:22.440
No one is speaking.

235
00:09:22.980 --> 00:09:26.240
In fact, people are signaling to each other to keep quiet.

236
00:09:26.420 --> 00:09:27.020
Don't talk.

237
00:09:27.020 --> 00:09:32.380
We had a fan question from Jacqueline K in San Diego who said, a few episodes back, you were

238
00:09:32.380 --> 00:09:35.700
talking about the longest silence on The Office.

239
00:09:36.100 --> 00:09:40.660
You both agreed that it was Jim and Pam on the boat in Boo's Cruise.

240
00:09:40.660 --> 00:09:45.520
But wouldn't this cold open technically be the episode with the longest silence?

241
00:09:45.900 --> 00:09:46.720
Well done.

242
00:09:46.920 --> 00:09:47.760
Yes, Jacqueline.

243
00:09:48.040 --> 00:09:48.840
Well done, Jacqueline.

244
00:09:49.240 --> 00:09:50.000
I timed it.

245
00:09:50.100 --> 00:09:51.640
It's one minute and 22 seconds.

246
00:09:51.720 --> 00:09:52.000
Oh, yeah.

247
00:09:52.160 --> 00:09:52.740
This is it.

248
00:09:52.860 --> 00:09:53.520
This is it.

249
00:09:53.780 --> 00:09:58.840
This cold open reminded me a lot of earlier seasons when the whole bullpen would get

250
00:09:58.840 --> 00:10:00.140
in on some prank together.

251
00:10:00.360 --> 00:10:00.660
Mm-hmm.

252
00:10:01.060 --> 00:10:01.820
I loved it.

253
00:10:02.000 --> 00:10:02.140
Yeah.

254
00:10:02.780 --> 00:10:07.840
Well, Andy's having a really hard time during this game of being silent because he sees

255
00:10:07.840 --> 00:10:11.920
a raccoon eating a burger out of a paper bag in the parking lot.

256
00:10:12.060 --> 00:10:16.280
He is trying so hard to, like, act out what he's seeing because this is crazy.

257
00:10:16.520 --> 00:10:18.000
He needs to share it with someone.

258
