00:40:40.350 --> 00:40:40.970
Let's do Tina.

1035
00:40:41.230 --> 00:40:41.650
Really?

1036
00:40:42.050 --> 00:40:42.770
I'm nervous.

1037
00:40:42.930 --> 00:40:43.490
Final answer?

1038
00:40:43.710 --> 00:40:44.110
Sure.

1039
00:40:45.110 --> 00:40:45.810
Aretha Franklin.

1040
00:40:46.350 --> 00:40:47.450
Sam had it.

1041
00:40:48.350 --> 00:40:50.230
How did you trust me?

1042
00:40:50.490 --> 00:40:50.710
Okay.

1043
00:40:51.250 --> 00:40:51.630
All right.

1044
00:40:51.790 --> 00:40:52.650
Last question.

1045
00:40:53.730 --> 00:41:00.350
What was the first and still only horror movie to win the Oscar for Best Picture?

1046
00:41:00.670 --> 00:41:01.270
The Shining?

1047
00:41:01.630 --> 00:41:02.150
ExorciSt.

1048
00:41:02.570 --> 00:41:03.250
Oh, ExorciSt.

1049
00:41:03.390 --> 00:41:03.590
Oh.

1050
00:41:04.870 --> 00:41:06.670
I know you guys are looking at me.

1051
00:41:07.070 --> 00:41:13.650
I don't know what horror movie won Best Picture first and still only won.

1052
00:41:13.850 --> 00:41:16.710
I think The Shining is a pretty good guess.

1053
00:41:17.070 --> 00:41:18.990
What about Poltergeist 2?

1054
00:41:19.430 --> 00:41:22.370
I don't know if that made it up to.

1055
00:41:22.570 --> 00:41:22.790
Yeah.

1056
00:41:23.450 --> 00:41:29.070
So I kind of knew for a while every Best Picture from 1970 to 1990.

1057
00:41:29.110 --> 00:41:33.010
It was a weird thing I did in college where I watched every movie.

1058
00:41:33.950 --> 00:41:35.710
And I can't remember.

1059
00:41:36.010 --> 00:41:38.530
Is this when you decided not to choose a calculator?

1060
00:41:38.730 --> 00:41:39.630
Is it the same time?

1061
00:41:39.810 --> 00:41:41.730
No, this was a different time period.

1062
00:41:41.730 --> 00:41:44.790
But you can tell I was a lot of fun in college.

1063
00:41:44.930 --> 00:41:47.510
That's what I did instead of attending parties.

1064
00:41:48.750 --> 00:41:49.150
All right.

1065
00:41:49.310 --> 00:41:50.030
Final answer.

1066
00:41:50.630 --> 00:41:51.990
I like The Shining.

1067
00:41:52.390 --> 00:41:52.610
Okay.

1068
00:41:52.750 --> 00:41:53.270
We can do The Shining.

1069
00:41:53.310 --> 00:41:54.450
Stanley Kubrick, right?

1070
00:41:54.570 --> 00:41:54.850
Yeah.

1071
00:41:55.230 --> 00:41:55.430
Okay.

1072
00:41:55.970 --> 00:41:58.410
The answer is Silence of the Lens.

1073
00:41:58.770 --> 00:41:58.870
Oh.

1074
00:42:01.400 --> 00:42:01.880
Okay.

1075
00:42:02.000 --> 00:42:04.480
That concludes our Office Ladies trivia.

1076
00:42:04.480 --> 00:42:06.760
Trivia, I hope you played along with us at home.

1077
00:42:07.180 --> 00:42:09.520
Oh, I'm sure people at home were screaming at their radios.

1078
00:42:09.820 --> 00:42:10.140
Horribly?

1079
00:42:10.600 --> 00:42:12.300
I think we're a Just for Fun team.

1080
00:42:12.580 --> 00:42:12.780
Sorry.

1081
00:42:12.920 --> 00:42:13.080
Yeah.

1082
00:42:14.940 --> 00:42:16.760
We're a Just for Otters team.

1083
00:42:16.920 --> 00:42:17.100
Yeah.

1084
00:42:17.360 --> 00:42:17.440
Yeah.

1085
00:42:17.440 --> 00:42:19.420
Oh, that was fun, Ange.

1086
00:42:19.960 --> 00:42:25.080
Well, speaking of trivia questions and how you come up with trivia questions, Heather H.

1087
00:42:25.220 --> 00:42:29.440
from California wants to know, how did they write this episode?

1088
00:42:29.440 --> 00:42:35.020
Did they have to work backwards and tailor questions to the teams?

1089
00:42:35.340 --> 00:42:38.580
Were there any good questions or answers that didn't make it?

1090
00:42:38.980 --> 00:42:43.920
Well, I asked Steve Healy, our writer, and he said that Heather is exactly right.

1091
00:42:44.120 --> 00:42:47.980
They reverse engineered the script because they wanted the Einsteins to win.

1092
00:42:48.580 --> 00:42:51.300
And they said, of course, Oscar is very sharp.

1093
00:42:51.400 --> 00:42:56.320
And so we wanted to see him winning a little bit because they felt that it would further

1094
00:42:56.320 --> 00:43:01.780
torture him if he was beat by just a little bit by the Einsteins.

1095
00:43:01.780 --> 00:43:06.240
And Steve also said there's probably a file with dozens more questions somewhere.

1096
00:43:06.400 --> 00:43:09.660
And he went through all his digital clutter looking for it.

1097
00:43:09.720 --> 00:43:12.460
And he said, I am so bummed that I couldn't find it for you.

1098
00:43:12.680 --> 00:43:18.100
I did love that one of the questions the Einsteins missed was the question about Albert Einstein.

1099
00:43:18.340 --> 00:43:18.820
I know.

1100
00:43:19.000 --> 00:43:20.360
That was an amazing joke.

1101
00:43:20.780 --> 00:43:26.840
Well, listen, meanwhile, back in Florida, Gabe has explained to Dwight that Bill, our COO,

1102
00:43:26.940 --> 00:43:28.440
will not be available.

1103
00:43:28.440 --> 00:43:30.800
He's putting out a fire nine miles away.

1104
00:43:30.800 --> 00:43:33.400
Gabe is going to listen to Dwight's pitch.

1105
00:43:33.760 --> 00:43:34.700
The toilet.

1106
00:43:35.220 --> 00:43:36.700
He's gone to the toilet.

1107
00:43:36.880 --> 00:43:37.620
He has.

1108
00:43:38.080 --> 00:43:40.420
And Dwight knows it, but he still commits.

1109
00:43:40.880 --> 00:43:46.100
Well, listen, it becomes very clear to Dwight very quickly that Gabe is just flushing him.

1110
00:43:46.400 --> 00:43:48.740
But he says, I'm a crap too big to flush.

1111
00:43:49.220 --> 00:43:53.240
And he demands that Gabe take him to Robert California's home.

1112
00:43:53.360 --> 00:43:53.560
Yeah.

1113
00:43:53.800 --> 00:43:54.880
We'll get to it.

1114
00:43:55.320 --> 00:44:00.040
First, we need to discuss what's happening at the trivia night and the controversy that was

1115
00:44:00.040 --> 00:44:03.540
created by one of our teams, the Quirinstein Bears.

1116
00:44:04.000 --> 00:44:05.600
Is that your thing, Ange?

1117
00:44:05.940 --> 00:44:06.860
I don't know.

1118
00:44:07.140 --> 00:44:08.780
I think mine is different.

1119
00:44:09.140 --> 00:44:10.480
Oh, we have different tidbits.

1120
00:44:10.600 --> 00:44:13.780
When we got here this morning, we thought we had the same surprise.

1121
00:44:14.120 --> 00:44:15.400
Now I'm very interested.

1122
00:44:15.400 --> 00:44:15.660
Me go firSt.

1123
00:44:15.860 --> 00:44:16.660
Me go first?

1124
00:44:16.860 --> 00:44:17.060
Yeah.

1125
00:44:17.400 --> 00:44:17.640
Okay.

1126
00:44:17.860 --> 00:44:22.000
Well, we're going to get an update on how the teams are in their standings.

1127
00:44:22.120 --> 00:44:25.260
The host is going to give the teams in order of their points.

1128
00:44:25.640 --> 00:44:30.780
And as he announces each team, you get to see a shot of that team.

1129
00:44:31.140 --> 00:44:32.580
Oh, it is the same.

1130
00:44:32.760 --> 00:44:33.580
It is the same.

1131
00:44:33.600 --> 00:44:33.940
It is?

1132
00:44:34.040 --> 00:44:34.540
Is this about a picture?

1133
00:44:34.720 --> 00:44:36.120
Is this about someone on a team?

1134
00:44:36.340 --> 00:44:36.780
Yes.

1135
00:44:36.940 --> 00:44:40.080
There is a moment where we cut to the Quirinstein Bears.

1136
00:44:40.080 --> 00:44:42.680
This is where all the guys act like bears.

1137
00:44:43.000 --> 00:44:49.140
The third guy from the left, wearing a green plaid shirt, set off a fan reaction so big

1138
00:44:49.140 --> 00:44:54.880
that NBC had to make a press release because people thought that this man was Steve Carell

1139
00:44:54.880 --> 00:44:56.380
in disguise.

1140
00:44:56.700 --> 00:44:56.880
Yep.

1141
00:44:57.040 --> 00:44:59.080
Making a cameo on the episode.

1142
00:44:59.340 --> 00:45:01.600
Sam, Cassie, I have a picture.

1143
00:45:01.920 --> 00:45:05.880
Yes, fans thought this was an uncredited cameo by Steve.

1144
00:45:06.060 --> 00:45:06.680
Oh, my God.

1145
00:45:08.120 --> 00:45:09.420
It looks like Robin WilliaMs.

1146
00:45:12.320 --> 00:45:16.760
We will put a picture up in Office Ladies Pod because for the third time in this episode,

1147
00:45:16.920 --> 00:45:20.240
once a fan pointed something out to me, I became obsessed with it.

1148
00:45:20.720 --> 00:45:22.640
It was such a crazy thing.

1149
00:45:22.780 --> 00:45:23.620
Did you read about it?

1150
00:45:23.640 --> 00:45:24.760
I did read about it.

1151
00:45:24.880 --> 00:45:29.240
And sorry, the way you set it up, I thought maybe the Quirinstein Bears had cheated or something.

1152
00:45:29.700 --> 00:45:31.340
Or the math was wrong.

1153
00:45:31.420 --> 00:45:34.060
And then people were like, they could be in the top three or whatever.

1154
00:45:34.260 --> 00:45:36.060
But yes, I did read about this.

1155
00:45:36.060 --> 00:45:38.500
There was a whole scuttlebutt after the episode aired.

1156
00:45:38.720 --> 00:45:42.260
People were all over the message boards thinking they spotted Steve.

1157
00:45:42.740 --> 00:45:48.620
I pulled one of the comments from Pat M who said, quote, I think it was Steve because first of all,

1158
00:45:48.740 --> 00:45:51.760
it looks like him with a fake beard and fake eyebrows.

1159
00:45:52.020 --> 00:45:57.980
Plus, later on the show, when they showed that table again, he's the only member of the group that isn't seen.

1160
00:45:58.380 --> 00:46:04.620
I think this is an indication that Steve came to visit, did the cameo for one day, but didn't hang for the whole filming.

1161
00:46:04.620 --> 00:46:06.340
This was the conspiracy.

1162
00:46:06.680 --> 00:46:15.500
I love that in this scenario, Steve comes to the set, makes the effort to come, but then dons a fake beard and eyebrows?

1163
00:46:15.880 --> 00:46:16.220
Mm-hmm.

1164
00:46:16.700 --> 00:46:17.900
This is a lot of effort.

1165
00:46:18.080 --> 00:46:21.580
To sit in and just act like a bear for one shot.

1166
00:46:21.740 --> 00:46:23.460
And then he's like, that was fun.

1167
00:46:23.580 --> 00:46:24.240
I'm out of here.

1168
00:46:24.480 --> 00:46:26.220
I really wish he would have now.

1169
00:46:26.220 --> 00:46:34.240
Well, this really blew up so much so that NBC gave a quote to the Washington Post saying, it was not Steve Carell.

1170
00:46:35.640 --> 00:46:37.920
They had to, like, announce it.

1171
00:46:38.100 --> 00:46:38.880
Oh, my gosh.

1172
00:46:39.140 --> 00:46:40.160
That is so funny.

1173
00:46:40.640 --> 00:46:45.440
Well, you know, when they list the top three teams, I became very curious about what the other teams were.

1174
00:46:45.520 --> 00:46:47.040
There's a lot of other teaMs.

1175
00:46:47.300 --> 00:46:48.540
Do they have names?

1176
00:46:48.880 --> 00:46:54.200
I went to the shooting draft because I figured this was something the writers would have had a lot of fun with.

1177
00:46:54.200 --> 00:46:56.540
And there are lots of other team names.

1178
00:46:56.740 --> 00:46:57.580
Oh, let's hear them.

1179
00:46:57.800 --> 00:46:58.160
Yes.

1180
00:46:58.380 --> 00:47:03.480
In the script, the trivia host had a whole rundown of all the scores.

1181
00:47:03.560 --> 00:47:04.760
It was a bigger speech.

1182
00:47:04.920 --> 00:47:05.260
Oh.

1183
00:47:05.400 --> 00:47:07.020
And this is what he would have said.

1184
00:47:07.680 --> 00:47:09.520
The Querenstein Bears was seven.

1185
00:47:10.000 --> 00:47:14.520
Joey Trivia-ani, get it, was seven.

1186
00:47:14.840 --> 00:47:17.460
The Impish Impresarios with three.

1187
00:47:17.880 --> 00:47:20.140
Two Broke Dorks with six.

1188
00:47:20.480 --> 00:47:24.000
Late Onset Bieber Fever with five.

1189
00:47:24.940 --> 00:47:26.880
Dunder Mifflin A-Team with four.

1190
00:47:27.180 --> 00:47:28.780
DM Backup Team with three.

1191
00:47:29.440 --> 00:47:32.020
Jason Sogakis, zero.

1192
00:47:32.820 --> 00:47:33.020
Oh.

1193
00:47:33.380 --> 00:47:34.480
Einstein's with eight.

1194
00:47:34.660 --> 00:47:37.040
And The Ladies Gaga with five.

1195
00:47:37.420 --> 00:47:37.840
Ah.

1196
00:47:38.460 --> 00:47:39.140
All right.

1197
00:47:39.140 --> 00:47:41.900
We got a fan question from Alexa P.

1198
00:47:42.020 --> 00:47:43.560
in Oceanside, New York, who said,

1199
00:47:43.780 --> 00:47:44.620
Hi, Jenna and Angela.

1200
00:47:44.800 --> 00:47:48.720
I am 15 years old and I've been listening to Office Ladies pretty much since it started.

1201
00:47:49.140 --> 00:47:54.040
For this episode, I have always wondered why isn't Kelly on the Dunder Mifflin A-Team?

1202
00:47:54.200 --> 00:48:01.340
When she clearly knows more about pop culture than anyone in the office, I feel like she would be my first choice to have on my team for a trivia night.

1203
00:48:01.880 --> 00:48:02.220
Same.

1204
00:48:02.540 --> 00:48:03.920
Alexa, I agree with you.

1205
00:48:04.000 --> 00:48:05.740
And this is exactly what Andy is discovering.

1206
00:48:06.080 --> 00:48:11.640
He has completely underestimated the people in his office and their use in a trivia night.

1207
00:48:12.160 --> 00:48:15.500
I mean, Meredith is about to throw down some great answers.

1208
00:48:15.800 --> 00:48:17.180
Meredith is going to get one right.

1209
00:48:17.460 --> 00:48:23.460
I mean, Aaron, who Meredith calls Princess Ding Dong, is going to get Flying Jib.

1210
00:48:24.080 --> 00:48:26.740
I mean, you know, I wouldn't have got that.

1211
00:48:27.120 --> 00:48:33.540
I did love when they get to the speed round and their, like, first team to ring in gets to answer.

1212
00:48:33.820 --> 00:48:37.100
And so Aaron rings in first and she's like, guys, I did my part.

1213
00:48:37.200 --> 00:48:37.920
What's the answer?

1214
00:48:38.260 --> 00:48:38.310
I don't know.

1215
00:48:38.420 --> 00:48:39.520
That was a very funny joke.

1216
00:48:39.700 --> 00:48:39.880
Yeah.

1217
00:48:40.360 --> 00:48:45.820
Well, I think we should head back to Florida because Dwight is going to arrive at Robert's condo.

1218
00:48:46.380 --> 00:48:50.100
This was an apartment complex in North Hollywood that was very close to the federal.

1219
00:48:50.100 --> 00:48:54.180
Steve Burgess told me we shot at the federal during the day one day.

1220
00:48:54.360 --> 00:48:57.400
And then after we finished, we moved over to this condo complex.

1221
00:48:58.360 --> 00:48:58.480
Yeah.

1222
00:48:58.680 --> 00:49:05.600
Dwight and Gabe are going to arrive and Robert California is having a wrestling lesson, I guess.

1223
00:49:06.060 --> 00:49:06.460
Yeah.

1224
00:49:06.700 --> 00:49:08.360
A wrestling workout.

1225
00:49:08.680 --> 00:49:08.840
Yeah.

1226
00:49:09.020 --> 00:49:12.100
With Stu, who was played by Peter Gannon.

1227
00:49:12.600 --> 00:49:15.620
I wanted to know, is this person a real wrestler?

1228
00:49:15.960 --> 00:49:16.240
Mm-hmm.

1229
00:49:16.400 --> 00:49:18.360
You know, you get cast in a role.

1230
00:49:18.780 --> 00:49:18.940
Yeah.

1231
00:49:18.940 --> 00:49:22.020
And all he does is, like, wrestle real hard.

1232
00:49:22.280 --> 00:49:24.060
I was like, what's the story?

1233
00:49:24.260 --> 00:49:26.000
I mean, he looked like he knew what he was doing.

1234
00:49:26.160 --> 00:49:34.560
Also, there's nothing funnier to me than what Robert looked like because he was at first kind of, like, he looked like a turtle on his back.

1235
00:49:34.700 --> 00:49:34.860
Yeah.

1236
00:49:34.860 --> 00:49:38.360
You know, he was trying to, like, flip the other guy over and flailing about.

1237
00:49:38.620 --> 00:49:40.760
And that's a very small space, you guys.

1238
00:49:40.960 --> 00:49:46.900
Imagine watching two grown men wrestling as hard as they can but just, like, one foot in front of you.

1239
00:49:46.900 --> 00:49:50.480
Yeah, like, between a couch and a coffee table they've moved.

1240
00:49:50.480 --> 00:49:56.440
Well, I found Peter Gannon's resume, and it does not look like he is a wrestler.

1241
00:49:56.440 --> 00:50:07.920
He listed many special skills, which were martial arts, taekwondo, boxing, scuba diving, horseback riding, golf, baseball, skiing, both water and snow.

1242
00:50:07.920 --> 00:50:12.040
So I reached out to Steve Burgess, and Steve Burgess says, no.

1243
00:50:12.360 --> 00:50:20.440
He is an actor they hired, and both Peter and James met with our stunt coordinator, Tom DeWire, and they just spent the morning wrestling.

1244
00:50:20.780 --> 00:50:25.500
And they came up with this wrestling choreography to make sure everyone would be safe in the scene.

1245
00:50:25.760 --> 00:50:27.040
That just blew my mind.

1246
00:50:27.220 --> 00:50:28.140
I don't know why.

1247
00:50:28.140 --> 00:50:28.800
Yeah.

1248
00:50:29.020 --> 00:50:29.960
What a crazy job to get.

1249
00:50:30.020 --> 00:50:30.400
I know.

1250
00:50:30.600 --> 00:50:31.360
What'd you do today?

1251
00:50:31.560 --> 00:50:33.780
I wrestled James Spader for most of the morning.

1252
00:50:33.800 --> 00:50:36.360
I didn't have any lines, but that was my job.

1253
00:50:36.680 --> 00:50:38.200
Well, I have a question for you.

1254
00:50:38.320 --> 00:50:38.600
Yes.

1255
00:50:38.800 --> 00:50:39.840
What happened to Gabe?

1256
00:50:40.200 --> 00:50:41.000
What do you mean?

1257
00:50:41.240 --> 00:50:42.940
I mean, Gabe walked in with Dwight.

1258
00:50:43.600 --> 00:50:44.160
Oh, yeah.

1259
00:50:44.160 --> 00:50:46.380
He said, yay, while they were wrestling.

1260
00:50:46.600 --> 00:50:46.920
What happened?

1261
00:50:46.920 --> 00:50:48.280
And then we never see him again.

1262
00:50:48.920 --> 00:50:49.900
Oh, my gosh.

1263
00:50:49.900 --> 00:50:50.860
You're so right.

1264
00:50:50.860 --> 00:50:52.340
I didn't even notice that.

1265
00:50:52.900 --> 00:50:58.660
Well, here is what I got obsessed with in this scene, and I'm glad I did because it really pays off.

1266
00:50:58.940 --> 00:51:09.720
When Robert is done wrestling, he offers Dwight an Oreo from a platter of Oreos because he says they haven't really improved on the Oreo, have they?

1267
00:51:10.180 --> 00:51:11.900
I did a deep dive on Oreos.

1268
00:51:12.080 --> 00:51:15.480
I love that he brought them out on a platter, and I love that you are doing a deep dive.

1269
00:51:15.640 --> 00:51:16.500
I'm going to sit back.

1270
00:51:16.500 --> 00:51:17.780
I can't wait to hear this.

1271
00:51:17.940 --> 00:51:21.980
Well, first of all, you know there's the Oreo and there's the Hydrox cookie.

1272
00:51:22.420 --> 00:51:27.280
They're both sandwich cookies that have, like, chocolate with a cream filling, right?

1273
00:51:27.700 --> 00:51:29.280
Which one do you think came first?

1274
00:51:29.280 --> 00:51:30.900
I've never heard of the Hydrox cookie.

1275
00:51:31.080 --> 00:51:32.020
I don't know what you're talking about.

1276
00:51:32.140 --> 00:51:32.280
What?

1277
00:51:32.600 --> 00:51:33.200
I don't know what you're talking about.

1278
00:51:33.200 --> 00:51:35.060
You've never heard of a Hydrox cookie?

1279
00:51:35.060 --> 00:51:38.340
It sounds like something you, like, clean, like, your sink with.

1280
00:51:38.600 --> 00:51:39.780
Put in the Hydrox cookie.

1281
00:51:39.900 --> 00:51:41.980
Sam, Cassie, have you heard of the Hydrox cookie?

1282
00:51:42.120 --> 00:51:44.700
Make sure you put in a Hydrox cookie in your dishwasher once a month.

1283
00:51:44.720 --> 00:51:46.640
Cassie has not heard of the Hydrox cookie.

1284
00:51:46.740 --> 00:51:46.980
Oh, my gosh.

1285
00:51:46.980 --> 00:51:47.640
Sam has.

1286
00:51:47.640 --> 00:51:54.000
All right, growing up, the Hydrox cookie was always kind of like, what?

1287
00:51:54.240 --> 00:51:56.700
Like, it was like the knockoff Oreo, right?

1288
00:51:56.960 --> 00:51:57.940
Sam is nodding.

1289
00:51:58.120 --> 00:51:58.500
Yes.

1290
00:51:58.980 --> 00:52:00.700
I'm going to blow your mind, Sam.

1291
00:52:01.140 --> 00:52:03.080
The Hydrox came firSt.

1292
00:52:03.460 --> 00:52:07.280
Oreo is a ripoff of the Hydrox.

1293
00:52:07.460 --> 00:52:07.820
Oh.

1294
00:52:08.620 --> 00:52:15.520
But it ended up just surpassing the Hydrox in popularity such that Cassie and Angela have

1295
00:52:15.520 --> 00:52:16.680
not even heard of it.

1296
00:52:17.200 --> 00:52:18.040
Why was it?

1297
00:52:18.120 --> 00:52:19.780
What did Oreo do differently?

1298
00:52:20.860 --> 00:52:23.360
I don't know, but it tastes better, right?

1299
00:52:23.520 --> 00:52:25.480
The Oreo tastes better than the Hydrox.

1300
00:52:25.520 --> 00:52:28.740
Well, listen, first error was naming it Hydrox.

1301
00:52:28.800 --> 00:52:30.140
The Hydrox is a terrible name.

1302
00:52:30.180 --> 00:52:31.460
Those sound like a cleaning solution.

1303
00:52:32.320 --> 00:52:40.800
All right, but Oreos were created in 1912, which was about three years after the Hydrox,

1304
00:52:41.020 --> 00:52:43.240
which was invented in 1908.

1305
00:52:43.900 --> 00:52:50.680
Oreos were invented in New York City, and they are so popular that the street in which the

1306
00:52:50.680 --> 00:52:56.040
first Nabisco factory is located, where the Oreo was first manufactured, is actually called

1307
00:52:56.040 --> 00:52:56.960
Oreo Way.

1308
00:52:57.180 --> 00:52:58.840
It's in Chelsea, New York.

1309
00:52:59.200 --> 00:53:00.740
This blew my mind.

1310
00:53:00.740 --> 00:53:04.720
It takes two hours to make one Oreo cookie.

1311
00:53:05.200 --> 00:53:07.960
That's how long it takes to make the whole thing.

1312
00:53:08.460 --> 00:53:14.780
Today, they produce 40 billion Oreo cookies per year, and if you stacked them all together,

1313
00:53:15.000 --> 00:53:16.960
they would circle the earth five times.

1314
00:53:17.060 --> 00:53:17.500
Wow.

1315
00:53:17.840 --> 00:53:18.440
Every year.

1316
00:53:18.700 --> 00:53:19.200
Wow.

1317
00:53:19.580 --> 00:53:25.480
Also, in 2011, the Oreo cookie received the Guinness World Record for the world's biggest

1318
00:53:25.480 --> 00:53:31.300
selling cookie with sales in excess of 500 billion since its debut.

1319
00:53:31.580 --> 00:53:37.460
The Oreo cookie, while it takes two hours to bake it, only contains 11 ingredients.

1320
00:53:37.980 --> 00:53:43.280
And Oreo cookies are kosher, and they're very popular with vegans because they contain no

1321
00:53:43.280 --> 00:53:44.400
animal products.

1322
00:53:44.860 --> 00:53:49.660
However, in their frequently asked questions, they do want you to know there is a risk of

1323
00:53:49.660 --> 00:53:53.900
cross-contamination because they are made in a facility that does make things with milk,

1324
00:53:54.020 --> 00:53:55.620
although there is no milk in the Oreo.

1325
00:53:56.360 --> 00:53:57.920
But here's the other crazy thing.

1326
00:53:58.780 --> 00:54:01.120
No one knows the origin of the name Oreo.

1327
00:54:01.820 --> 00:54:03.620
We don't know why it's called an Oreo.

1328
00:54:04.260 --> 00:54:05.700
Doesn't stand for anything.

1329
00:54:05.960 --> 00:54:11.380
There's a lot of speculation that maybe it has, like, roots in some Latin word.

1330
00:54:11.900 --> 00:54:12.760
But, yeah.

1331
00:54:13.080 --> 00:54:13.560
Hmm.

1332
00:54:13.680 --> 00:54:17.860
I was very excited when I did this deep dive, and at dinner, I announced it to my whole

1333
00:54:17.860 --> 00:54:18.380
family.

1334
00:54:18.560 --> 00:54:19.360
What'd they think?

1335
00:54:19.520 --> 00:54:21.160
They were fascinated by it.

1336
00:54:21.220 --> 00:54:26.360
My son has heard of the Hydrox, by the way, and he knew that the Hydrox came firSt.

1337
00:54:26.360 --> 00:54:27.560
No, he knew.

1338
00:54:27.800 --> 00:54:31.100
I was shocked that the Hydrox came firSt.

1339
00:54:31.400 --> 00:54:31.580
I knew.

1340
00:54:31.820 --> 00:54:34.420
I don't know, but he was like, Mom, everyone knows that.

1341
00:54:34.700 --> 00:54:36.660
Everyone knows the Hydrox came firSt.

1342
00:54:36.680 --> 00:54:37.800
I did not know that.

1343
00:54:37.980 --> 00:54:38.620
I know.

1344
00:54:39.120 --> 00:54:42.700
So, anyway, my family was like, where'd the name come from?

1345
00:54:42.860 --> 00:54:44.040
That was their big question.

1346
00:54:44.100 --> 00:54:45.060
I was like, no one knows.

1347
00:54:45.560 --> 00:54:46.400
Nobody knows.

1348
00:54:46.680 --> 00:54:46.920
Hmm.

1349
00:54:47.460 --> 00:54:51.500
That, my friends, is your Oreo cookie deep dive.

1350
00:54:51.680 --> 00:54:52.960
It's making me want an Oreo.

1351
00:54:53.400 --> 00:54:56.220
While I was doing this deep dive, I ate two Oreos.

1352
00:54:56.360 --> 00:54:58.980
And by the way, the gluten-free Oreo is delicious as well.

1353
00:54:59.500 --> 00:55:02.140
My favorite Oreo is the yellow one.

1354
00:55:02.580 --> 00:55:03.720
The lemon cream?

1355
00:55:04.220 --> 00:55:04.660
No.

1356
00:55:04.820 --> 00:55:05.620
Isn't there one that's just-

1357
00:55:05.620 --> 00:55:06.160
The vanilla one?

1358
00:55:06.240 --> 00:55:06.420
Yeah.

1359
00:55:06.720 --> 00:55:06.980
Mm-hmm.

1360
00:55:07.120 --> 00:55:07.900
Yeah, it's very good.

1361
00:55:08.100 --> 00:55:11.160
I like to put it in the fridge and eat it cold.

1362
00:55:11.540 --> 00:55:13.680
You like a cold vanilla Oreo.

1363
00:55:15.180 --> 00:55:15.620
Fascinating.

1364
00:55:15.860 --> 00:55:17.320
I'm bringing sexy back.

1365
00:55:17.900 --> 00:55:18.260
What?

1366
00:55:19.900 --> 00:55:23.800
Well, Robert is really trying to do the thing he set out to do, the Peter Principle.

1367
00:55:24.220 --> 00:55:24.640
Mm-hmm.

1368
00:55:24.640 --> 00:55:27.900
He's trying to get Dwight to stay right in the position he's in.

1369
00:55:28.080 --> 00:55:29.700
He's like, you don't want to live in Florida.

1370
00:55:29.940 --> 00:55:30.820
And you know what?

1371
00:55:30.960 --> 00:55:33.640
He said, I want to give you something.

1372
00:55:34.120 --> 00:55:39.960
He says that he wants to give Dwight his grandfather's medal, that his grandfather was

1373
00:55:39.960 --> 00:55:42.300
awarded for acts of courage and excellence.

1374
00:55:43.120 --> 00:55:47.480
But he pulls it out of a drawer that, I'm sorry, it looks like that catch-all drawer in your

1375
00:55:47.480 --> 00:55:47.860
kitchen.

1376
00:55:48.140 --> 00:55:48.360
It sure does.

1377
00:55:48.560 --> 00:55:49.780
It's got everything in it.

1378
00:55:49.920 --> 00:55:51.680
And this special medal, apparently.

1379
00:55:52.020 --> 00:55:52.120
Yeah.

1380
00:55:52.340 --> 00:55:53.380
And Dwight's like, you know what?

1381
00:55:53.380 --> 00:55:56.420
I don't need a trinket from a flea market, you know?

1382
00:55:56.540 --> 00:55:56.720
Yeah.

1383
00:55:57.160 --> 00:55:58.860
I think Robert has a lot of these medals.

1384
00:55:59.020 --> 00:56:00.480
I think this is his go-to drawer.

1385
00:56:00.480 --> 00:56:02.140
Well, this is the thing.

1386
00:56:02.520 --> 00:56:04.480
So the fan catch from Scott P.

1387
00:56:04.560 --> 00:56:07.580
in Tennessee said, did you notice that the medal that Robert

1388
00:56:07.580 --> 00:56:10.600
gives Dwight also appears framed at Gabe's desk?

1389
00:56:10.740 --> 00:56:11.620
We talked about this.

1390
00:56:11.760 --> 00:56:11.900
Yes.

1391
00:56:12.000 --> 00:56:15.760
When you see Gabe's desk throughout this episode, you can see it plain as day.

1392
00:56:15.760 --> 00:56:17.200
Thor M.

1393
00:56:17.400 --> 00:56:18.400
from Washington, D.C.

1394
00:56:18.560 --> 00:56:23.380
also spotted this and added that he feels like this really

1395
00:56:23.380 --> 00:56:28.500
shows the difference between Gabe and Dwight, that Gabe cherished this object and needed to

1396
00:56:28.500 --> 00:56:33.840
display it, you know, maybe to impress Robert or maybe to impress other people.

1397
00:56:34.020 --> 00:56:35.460
Or Stephanie in the lobby.

1398
00:56:35.820 --> 00:56:38.720
I mean, it's right there in his squirrel desk area.

1399
00:56:39.180 --> 00:56:42.520
Thor also said, I actually have that medal.

1400
00:56:42.920 --> 00:56:44.800
I was awarded it twice.

1401
00:56:44.800 --> 00:56:51.240
Oh, Thor said, humble brag, but the award that Robert is attempting to give Dwight is

1402
00:56:51.240 --> 00:56:52.860
an Army Achievement Medal.

1403
00:56:53.120 --> 00:56:59.420
And this adds another level of irony to the show that I love as a veteran, because an AAM

1404
00:56:59.420 --> 00:57:06.220
would never be given for bravery or valor, but it is more like an exemplary service medal,

1405
00:57:06.480 --> 00:57:09.840
like as a commemoration of your time in a military unit.

1406
00:57:09.840 --> 00:57:16.260
And they are given as a going away medal, as like a summary of all your achievements at your unit

1407
00:57:16.260 --> 00:57:18.440
before you move on to the next chapter.

1408
00:57:19.460 --> 00:57:24.840
So he thought it was very interesting that Robert is handing out these sort of going

1409
00:57:24.840 --> 00:57:25.600
away medals.

1410
00:57:25.960 --> 00:57:27.800
Take this medal and now go away.

1411
00:57:27.840 --> 00:57:28.160
Go away.

1412
00:57:28.340 --> 00:57:28.700
Exactly.

1413
00:57:29.120 --> 00:57:32.820
And in fact, he says to Dwight, I'll think of you if something comes up.

1414
00:57:32.920 --> 00:57:34.500
Now get the hell out of my condo.

1415
00:57:34.700 --> 00:57:35.020
Yes.

1416
00:57:35.520 --> 00:57:36.520
But you know what?

1417
00:57:36.680 --> 00:57:39.080
Dwight likes it when he talks straight to him.

1418
00:57:39.320 --> 00:57:39.440
Yeah.

1419
00:57:39.460 --> 00:57:42.760
When he says, I'm not giving you this job, but if something comes up that you're right

1420
00:57:42.760 --> 00:57:43.720
for, I'll tell you.

1421
00:57:44.080 --> 00:57:44.200
Right.

1422
00:57:44.220 --> 00:57:45.420
Dwight's like, I accept that.

1423
00:57:45.420 --> 00:57:46.360
That's enough for him.

1424
00:57:46.660 --> 00:57:46.760
Yeah.

1425
00:57:47.440 --> 00:57:50.040
Back at the bar, we're in the final round of trivia.

1426
00:57:50.380 --> 00:57:51.220
We are.

1427
00:57:51.380 --> 00:57:53.360
And I have a fun little background catch.

1428
00:57:53.880 --> 00:57:57.600
During this scene, you can see out the windows and it looks like night.

1429
00:57:57.900 --> 00:57:59.620
Is Steve there in a beard and eyebrows?

1430
00:57:59.860 --> 00:58:01.160
No, you never see him again.

1431
00:58:02.860 --> 00:58:04.700
But that was fakie night.

1432
00:58:04.940 --> 00:58:05.300
Oh.

1433
00:58:05.440 --> 00:58:06.280
It was really day.

1434
00:58:06.960 --> 00:58:10.080
Steve Healy said that we had to do day for night shooting.

1435
00:58:10.080 --> 00:58:17.160
And day for night shooting is when they have to cover the windows with like big black scriMs.

1436
00:58:17.320 --> 00:58:21.980
But then they also have to put enough light outside the window that it looks like nighttime.

1437
00:58:21.980 --> 00:58:23.420
They did a really good job.

1438
00:58:23.640 --> 00:58:27.440
I became slightly obsessed with looking out the windows and being like, that's not really

1439
00:58:27.440 --> 00:58:27.800
night.

1440
00:58:28.160 --> 00:58:31.200
You had so much to look at in the background in this episode.

1441
00:58:31.460 --> 00:58:32.180
I really did.

1442
00:58:32.380 --> 00:58:32.940
You were very busy.

1443
00:58:33.080 --> 00:58:33.580
I was.

1444
00:58:33.900 --> 00:58:36.280
Well, in this final round, things are getting heated.

1445
00:58:36.880 --> 00:58:38.980
The trivia host reads this question.

1446
00:58:39.160 --> 00:58:43.280
The standard American analog scale has a maximum capacity of what weight?

1447
00:58:43.480 --> 00:58:44.500
Kevin rings the bell.

1448
00:58:44.620 --> 00:58:45.320
300 pounds.

1449
00:58:45.540 --> 00:58:46.420
He knows the answer.

1450
00:58:46.580 --> 00:58:47.140
Knows the answer.

1451
00:58:47.300 --> 00:58:48.260
Einstein's get it.

1452
00:58:49.300 --> 00:58:50.420
Final question.

1453
00:58:51.080 --> 00:58:55.220
For all the cinephiles out there, put on your memory berets.

1454
00:58:55.520 --> 00:58:57.780
The final question is a doozy.

1455
00:58:57.960 --> 00:58:59.700
It's about a French film.

1456
00:59:00.220 --> 00:59:02.120
Oscar thinks he has this in the bag.

1457
00:59:02.260 --> 00:59:03.840
He's very confident with his answer.

1458
00:59:04.040 --> 00:59:05.660
But no, it's incorrect.

1459
00:59:05.660 --> 00:59:07.940
And who has the right answer but Kevin?

1460
00:59:08.540 --> 00:59:10.040
And the Einsteins win.

1461
00:59:10.560 --> 00:59:14.700
The Einsteins win because Kevin answered the last two questions correctly.

1462
00:59:15.240 --> 00:59:20.000
Kevin, the person that Andy forced off the A-team in the very beginning.

1463
00:59:20.660 --> 00:59:26.720
Kevin has a great talking head where he says, you know, maybe tonight was a fluke, but fluke

1464
00:59:26.720 --> 00:59:28.240
is the most common fish in the sea.

1465
00:59:28.840 --> 00:59:31.860
It's the fish you're most likely to catch when you go fishing.

1466
00:59:32.080 --> 00:59:34.000
There was a Kevin alt-talking head.

1467
00:59:34.000 --> 00:59:35.720
It's in Deleted Scenes.

1468
00:59:35.860 --> 00:59:37.840
And he talks about his level of education.

1469
00:59:38.340 --> 00:59:39.440
Here's what he said.

1470
00:59:39.600 --> 00:59:44.800
When I dropped out of school to watch more sports, a lot of people thought I was nuts.

1471
00:59:45.360 --> 00:59:47.200
Well, who's laughing now?

1472
00:59:47.980 --> 00:59:51.120
What's funny is that Kevin never answered a sports question.

1473
00:59:51.120 --> 00:59:51.640
I know.

1474
00:59:51.980 --> 00:59:52.840
I know.

1475
00:59:53.060 --> 00:59:53.440
I know.

1476
00:59:53.460 --> 00:59:55.960
But we found out he dropped out of school.

1477
00:59:56.500 --> 01:00:02.020
And then there's a deleted Oscar talking head where he sort of sums up the day.

1478
01:00:02.120 --> 01:00:03.240
It's pretty funny.

1479
01:00:03.420 --> 01:00:04.400
I'm going to read it to you.

1480
01:00:04.880 --> 01:00:06.440
It gets better, kids.

1481
01:00:06.760 --> 01:00:12.820
It gets so much better that one day your stupid co-workers will be excited to show up at your

1482
01:00:12.820 --> 01:00:14.980
gay bar and ruin your trivia night.

1483
01:00:15.680 --> 01:00:16.160
Oh, boy.

1484
01:00:16.280 --> 01:00:18.900
I don't think I want to be in that accounting clump tomorrow.

1485
01:00:19.300 --> 01:00:21.580
Hey, Angela had nothing to do with it.

1486
01:00:21.700 --> 01:00:22.100
That's right.

1487
01:00:22.100 --> 01:00:25.200
But it would be kind of icy between Kevin and Oscar.

1488
01:00:25.720 --> 01:00:29.940
So the tag for this episode is another trivia night.

1489
01:00:30.180 --> 01:00:33.040
The Einsteins have made it to the state championships.

1490
01:00:33.460 --> 01:00:36.140
The state championships in all of Pennsylvania.

1491
01:00:36.460 --> 01:00:37.300
That's insane.

1492
01:00:37.720 --> 01:00:41.380
The host of this trivia night is played by comedian Dan Levy.

1493
01:00:41.960 --> 01:00:43.700
He is hilarious, by the way.

1494
01:00:43.820 --> 01:00:46.420
And here's a crazy, odd coincidence.

1495
01:00:46.860 --> 01:00:49.480
Just one of those things how life comes back around.

1496
01:00:50.140 --> 01:00:53.400
Dan was my husband's roommate when they were in their 20s.

1497
01:00:53.520 --> 01:00:54.180
No way.

1498
01:00:54.380 --> 01:00:54.540
Yeah.

1499
01:00:54.840 --> 01:00:55.420
Oh, my gosh.

1500
01:00:55.420 --> 01:00:56.400
They were roommates.

1501
01:00:56.600 --> 01:00:58.120
They had met in an acting class.

1502
01:00:58.340 --> 01:00:59.180
They're still friends.

1503
01:00:59.640 --> 01:01:00.880
He is hilarious.

1504
01:01:01.080 --> 01:01:02.720
And he tours with John Mulaney.

1505
01:01:02.840 --> 01:01:03.580
He's so funny.

1506
01:01:03.820 --> 01:01:06.860
I'm going to put a little swipe up to Dan because he's fantastic.

1507
01:01:07.340 --> 01:01:10.120
Well, I remember him from his role in the TV show Enlightened.

1508
01:01:10.280 --> 01:01:11.140
He is awesome.

1509
01:01:11.380 --> 01:01:11.980
Oh, yeah.

1510
01:01:12.500 --> 01:01:14.620
Well, guys, that is trivia.

1511
01:01:15.100 --> 01:01:19.160
I want to give a big thank you to Steve Burgess and Steve Healy for all your tidbits.

1512
01:01:19.620 --> 01:01:21.220
You know, Angela, Steve Healy was so sweet.

1513
01:01:21.360 --> 01:01:25.340
I have to read you what he said at the end of our email chain talking about trivia.

1514
01:01:25.780 --> 01:01:27.940
He just said thank you to both of us.

1515
01:01:28.060 --> 01:01:32.520
And he said, anytime I think back on The Office, it's a deep wave of memories.

1516
01:01:32.960 --> 01:01:36.340
Some of them just of the late nights and stress and weird food.

1517
01:01:36.980 --> 01:01:39.320
But it's also so fun to remember.

1518
01:01:39.560 --> 01:01:44.320
And more than anything, I am grateful I was part of such a project that people love so much.

1519
01:01:44.720 --> 01:01:46.500
I think that sums it up.

1520
01:01:46.500 --> 01:01:48.040
Yeah, that's how I feel, too.

1521
01:01:48.260 --> 01:01:48.480
Yeah.

1522
01:01:48.480 --> 01:01:54.880
And a special thank you to my friend Brian Gattis for sharing with us what it was like being on set shooting those bar scenes.

1523
01:01:55.420 --> 01:01:57.040
Brian has been in a ton of things.

1524
01:01:57.260 --> 01:02:00.100
And you can check him out on Instagram at Built by Gattis.

1525
01:02:00.220 --> 01:02:01.780
He does his own ceramics, Jenna.

1526
01:02:02.380 --> 01:02:02.780
Really?

1527
01:02:02.780 --> 01:02:03.440
I know.

1528
01:02:04.020 --> 01:02:06.060
